-- title:
--   Germs at the cusp with p-divisible q-expansion are p-divisible
-- statement:
--   Fix $N\ge 1$ and a prime $p$ with $p\nmid N$, and write $R=\mathbb{Z}_{(p)}$ for the subring [`GaloisRep.ratLocalizedAt p`](def/GaloisRep_Flat.html#L8) of $\mathbb{Q}$ consisting of the rationals whose denominator is coprime to $p$. Let $c\colon X\to\operatorname{Spec}R$ be proper and smooth of relative dimension $1$ with $X$ integral. The data comprise: a curve model $M_0$ over $\mathbb{Q}$ of the field `modularFunctionFieldFull N` (the subfield of $\mathbb{Q}((q))$ generated by the expansions $q\mapsto j(q^d)$ for $d\mid N$), i.e. an integral proper smooth curve with function field identified with that field and closed points bijective to places, together with an isomorphism $e_0$ onto the generic fibre of $c$ over the structure morphism; a section $\varepsilon_\infty$ of $c$; a closed point $x_0$ of $M_0.C$ whose place is the $q$-adic cusp `cuspInftyFull N`; a $\mathbb{Q}$-point $y$ of the generic fibre lying on $\varepsilon_\infty$ and sending the closed point to $x_0$; and the geometric fibre over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ with its Galois equivariance, the liftings $\rho$ of $R$ into valuation subrings $A\subset\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ with $p\in A^{\mathrm{nonunits}}$, the fibre models over the residue fields, and the existence of degree- and order-preserving reduction maps on places compatible with points (these geometric and reduction hypotheses are summarised here). Let $s\in\operatorname{Spec}R$ be a closed point with $x_0$ specialising to $\varepsilon_\infty(s)$ in $X$. Assume given a ring homomorphism $\pi$ from the stalk of $X$ at $\varepsilon_\infty(s)$ to $R$ retracting the structure map, with $\ker(\pi)$ containing the kernel of the stalk map of $\varepsilon_\infty$ at $s$, and an element $t$ of that stalk with $\pi t=0$ and $\ker\pi\subseteq (t)+(\ker\pi)^2$. Assume $u\in R[[q]]$ has $u(0)=0$ and unit linear coefficient, and that the $q$-expansion of $t$ — obtained by specialising to $x_0$, applying the stalk map of $e_0$ followed by the first projection, passing to the function field and back along $M_0.\mathrm{ffEquiv}^{-1}$ into $\mathbb{Q}((q))$ — is the image of $u$ under $R\to\mathbb{Q}$. Then for any $z$ in the stalk and any $P\in R[[q]]$ whose associated $q$-expansion of $z$ equals the image of $p\cdot P$, the element $z$ is $p$ times some $z'$ in the stalk, where $p$ acts through the germ at $\varepsilon_\infty(s)$ of the global section of $X$ pulled back from $p\in R$.
--
--   This is the saturation step at the cusp: since the formal neighbourhood of the cusp section is $\operatorname{Spf}R[[q]]$, with $q$ supplied as a parameter by the element $t$, divisibility of a germ by $p$ is detected on $q$-expansions. It feeds the statement on Kähler differentials at the cusp, [`ModularCurve.exists_eq_smul_of_diffQExpBar_eq_ofPowerSeries_smul_of_kaehlerH0_of_ratCurveModel_of_cuspSection_compat_of_neZero`](thm.html#ModularCurve.exists_eq_smul_of_diffQExpBar_eq_ofPowerSeries_smul_of_kaehlerH0_of_ratCurveModel_of_cuspSection_compat_of_neZero).
-- source:
